`tilepics[mapspot]` is null → mapspot out of range? `this.tilePos` could go out of bounds. In tileDisplay: `mapspot = this.tilePos + loopy*MAP_COLS + loopx`. tilePos starts at (MAP_ROWS - MAP_ONSCREEN)*MAP_COLS = (150-8)*9 = 1278. As scrolling, tilePos -= MAP_COLS each scroll until <= 0. But wait — the scroll decrements tilePos by MAP_COLS each time tileYoff > 0. Hmm, in the earlier tests we only ran ~600 frames in wave 0. Now the loop runs across many waves, and wave 8 (MAP9G1_MAP) may have a different map size — maybe the map has fewer tiles or different scroll behavior, causing tilePos to go negative or beyond.

Actually wait — the error is `this.tilepics[mapspot]` is null meaning tilepics is null, not mapspot out of bounds. `this.tilepics` is Uint32Array initialized in tileSetup. But tileDisplay accesses this.tilepics[mapspot]. If `this.tilepics` were null... Hmm, "Cannot read properties of null (reading '0')" means `this.tilepics` is null (the thing being indexed is null). So this.tilepics is null at that point. When would tilepics be null? resetGlobals sets `this.tilepics = null`. startWave → loadMap → tileSetup sets tilepics. But wait — in my loop, when between expires I call g.startWave(wave) which calls loadMap + tileSetup. That sets tilepics. So how is it null?

Unless loadMap for wave 8 (MAP9G1_MAP) throws and tileSetup never runs? No, it would throw earlier.

Actually — `this.tilepics` could be null if `this.tileSetup()` was never called for the current wave, OR if `startWave` itself is failing. Let me check the order in startWave: `this.loadMap(wave); this.tileSetup();`. tileSetup sets tilepics = new Uint32Array(MAP_SIZE). So it's set.

Wait — but maybe mapspot is out of bounds causing... no, indexing a Uint32Array with out-of-range returns undefined, not throws on the array. The error says "Cannot read properties of null (reading '0')" — the receiver `this.tilepics` is null.

Hmm, let me look at tileDisplay again — line 759: `const item = this.tilepics[mapspot];`. So this.tilepics is null. How? 

OH WAIT. Look at tileDisplay scroll logic:
```
this.tileYoff++;
if (this.tileYoff > 0) {
  if (this.last_tile && this.tileYoff >= 0) { this.tileYoff = 0; this.scroll_flag = false; }
  else { this.tileYoff -= MAP_BLOCKSIZE; this.tilePos -= MAP_COLS; }
  if (this.tilePos <= 0) { this.tilePos = 0; this.last_tile = true; }
}
```
tilePos never goes below 0. mapspot = tilePos + ... up to tilePos + 8*9+8 = tilePos+80 ≤ 80. tilepics is 1350 long. Fine.

So why null? Let me reconsider. Maybe startWave for wave 8 throws inside loadMap before tileSetup, leaving tilepics null from resetGlobals... Actually no — loadMap runs before tileSetup; if loadMap throws, tilepics stays null (from previous wave's value, which is set). Hmm.

Wait, actually maybe the issue is `MAP9G1_MAP` doesn't exist! Sector 1 = waves 1..9? Actually sector 1 has 9 waves → maps MAP1G1..MAP9G1? Let me check the map names. The original game: sector 1 = waves 1-9, sector 2 = 10-18, sector 3 = 19-27. So MAP9G1 exists. But maybe the shareware data only has up to MAP8G1? Let me check what MAP items exist.

Actually wait, the error could also be from wave 8 when loadMap finds no 'MAP9G1_MAP' and throws... but the error is in tileDisplay not loadMap. Let me check what maps exist in glb1.

